# Server config for chipseq.app. cyon runs LiteSpeed, which reads .htaccess and
# implements mod_headers, so this is the ordinary Apache syntax. GitHub Pages
# ignores the file entirely.
#
# Only the things a default server gets wrong for this app. Everything else is
# deliberately left alone - the service worker owns caching once installed, so
# there is very little for HTTP to decide.
#
# One LiteSpeed behaviour shapes how this is verified: it ignores directives it
# does not recognise SILENTLY, where Apache would refuse to start. A typo here
# therefore produces no error and no effect. That is why the deploy workflow
# asserts the headers this file is supposed to produce, instead of assuming
# that shipping the file was the same as it working.

# Without this the manifest is served as application/octet-stream, the browser
# ignores it, and the app is simply not installable - with nothing in the
# console to say why. This is the single most important line in the file.
AddType application/manifest+json .webmanifest

# Belt and braces: Apache knows these, but a mis-typed .js is a blank page.
AddType text/javascript .js
AddType image/svg+xml .svg

# The whole repository is mirrored here, so without this Apache would offer
# browsable listings of tests/, server/ and demos/. The source is public
# anyway; a directory index is still an invitation nobody asked to send.
Options -Indexes

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  # The worker script decides when a new version is even noticed. It is fetched
  # with updateViaCache:'none', which already bypasses the HTTP cache, but a
  # proxy in between has no idea about that - so say it out loud.
  <Files "sw.js">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </Files>
  # Same reasoning for the entry point: everything else is reached through it,
  # and it is the one file that must never be a stale copy.
  <Files "index.html">
    Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
  </Files>
</IfModule>

# .app is HSTS-preloaded at the TLD, so browsers refuse plain HTTP before a
# request is ever made. No redirect needed, and the service worker's
# secure-context requirement is satisfied by construction.
